To design a secure login form, you must prioritize server-side validation and secure transport via HTTPS. Below is a foundational implementation using HTML5 and the Fetch API for AJAX.

* Use the following HTML structure for your form:
<form id="loginForm">
  <label for="username">Username:</label>
  <input type="text" id="username" name="username" required>
  <label for="password">Password:</label>
  <input type="password" id="password" name="password" autocomplete="current-password" required>
  <button type="submit">Login</button>
</form>
<div id="message"></div>

* Implement the AJAX logic to handle the POST request:
document.getElementById('loginForm').addEventListener('submit', async (e) => {
  e.preventDefault();
  const formData = new FormData(e.target);
  const response = await fetch('/login-endpoint', {
    method: 'POST',
    body: formData
  });
  const result = await response.json();
  document.getElementById('message').innerText = result.message || 'Error occurred.';
});

* Always implement CSRF tokens within the form to prevent cross-site request forgery attacks.
* Ensure your backend handles password hashing with secure algorithms like Argon2 or bcrypt.
* Validate all inputs on the server side, as client-side validation is easily bypassed by malicious actors.

